<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Gamio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Gamio was a Mexican anthropologist and archaeologist, often called the father of modern Mexican anthropology. His study of the population of the Teotihuacan Valley, published in 1922, combined archaeology, history and ethnography, and his excavations exposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-feathered-serpent">Feathered Serpent Pyramid</a>. He was a leading advocate of indigenismo and later studied Mexican migration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-gamio.jpg" alt="Manuel Gamio" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (National Photo Company) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Gamio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
